# Copy this file to `.kamal/secrets` before the first deploy.
#
#     cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
#
# `.kamal/secrets` is in .gitignore. This example isn't: it contains no secret,
# it just says where to get them. Where the deploy *goes* — the host, the user,
# the music — is a separate file: see `.kamal/deploy.env.example`.
#
# The registry runs on the server itself, on your LAN, with no real auth, so
# this password is a formality, not a secret: Kamal refuses a non-localhost
# registry without one, and the registry accepts anything. It stays here in the
# clear on purpose.
KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=kamal
#
# The master key lives in config/master.key, which is gitignored. If you cloned
# the repo onto a new machine, copy it from one that already has it; if this is
# a fresh install of your own, `bin/rails credentials:edit` writes you one.
# Without it, the app won't boot in production.
RAILS_MASTER_KEY=$(cat config/master.key)
#
# The certificate Mediateca is served under, and its key — read only when you set
# MEDIATECA_TLS_HOST. Kamal hands them to kamal-proxy; they never reach the app.
# Both, or neither: Kamal refuses one without the other.
#
# `.kamal/tls/` is gitignored, and the key in it is a private key: it is a file
# only because the thing that deploys has to read it without a human present —
# the CI runner is on the NAS, and a keychain there would need somebody to unlock
# it. Keep it 600, and keep it off any machine that doesn't deploy.
#
# With no certificate these are empty, nothing asks for them, and the deploy is
# the plain HTTP it always was. The README says where they come from.
MEDIATECA_TLS_CERT=$(cat .kamal/tls/cert.pem 2>/dev/null)
MEDIATECA_TLS_KEY=$(cat .kamal/tls/key.pem 2>/dev/null)
#
# Last.fm. Without these there is no Last.fm in the app at all: no menu item, no
# scrobbles, no import, and the app is whole without them. An API account gives
# you both — https://www.last.fm/api/account/create — and its callback URL is
# `https://<your host>/scrobbler/callback`.
#
# The environment first, the keychain second — because this file is copied verbatim
# onto every machine that deploys, and they do not all keep their secrets in the
# same place.
#
# On the NAS, where the CI runner deploys with nobody present to unlock anything,
# they come from `deploy.env`, which is that machine's and is in no checkout.
# On a Mac, where a human is at the keyboard, they come out of the keychain and
# never touch a file at all:
#
#     security add-generic-password -a lastfm -s mediateca-lastfm-key    -w
#     security add-generic-password -a lastfm -s mediateca-lastfm-secret -w
#
# Set neither and there is simply no Last.fm in the app, which is a deploy that
# works — not one that stops.
LASTFM_API_KEY=${LASTFM_API_KEY:-$(security find-generic-password -a lastfm -s mediateca-lastfm-key -w 2>/dev/null)}
LASTFM_API_SECRET=${LASTFM_API_SECRET:-$(security find-generic-password -a lastfm -s mediateca-lastfm-secret -w 2>/dev/null)}
